<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norodom Sihanouk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/2012">2012</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norodom Sihanouk was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, placed on the throne by the French in 1941. He won independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1953, abdicated in 1955 to lead politics as prince and head of state, and kept Cambodia neutral during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a> deposed him in 1970. Allied in exile with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>, he was held under house arrest by them after 1975. He returned as king in 1993 and abdicated in 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk.jpg" alt="Norodom Sihanouk" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (unknown, Abaca Press claims ownership but claim is doubtful) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Norodom_Sihanouk_in_1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Norodom Sihanouk was the only child of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-suramarit">Norodom Suramarit</a> and Princess Sisowath Kossamak, daughter of King Sisowath Monivong. His parents followed the advice of the Royal Court Astrologer, who warned that he risked dying young if raised under parental care, and placed him with Kossamak's grandmother. When Monivong died in 1941, Sihanouk became king under French colonial rule. After the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in August 1945, nationalist forces loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-ngoc-thanh">Son Ngoc Thanh</a> staged a coup; when the French returned in October, Thanh was dismissed, and a modus vivendi of January 1946 granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> autonomy within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 2 March 1955 Sihanouk suddenly abdicated in favour of his father, saying he wished to escape the intrigues of palace life and reach ordinary people. According to Osborne, the step freed him to pursue politics while keeping the deference he had enjoyed as king. His party, Sangkum, won the elections that year and he became prime minister. His changes included votes for women, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> as the sole official language, and policy-making powers vested in the prime minister rather than the king. In 1956 he began a national programme of Buddhist socialism. After Suramarit died on 3 April 1960, a referendum approved a new post of Head of State, to which Sihanouk was appointed on 14 June 1960 while remaining Sangkum's leader.</p><p>In March 1970, while Sihanouk was abroad, a coup brought a military government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a>. He announced a resistance movement on 23 March and encouraged Cambodians to fight; revered by the peasantry, his endorsement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> had immediate effects. He returned as figurehead head of state after their victory in 1975, resigned in 1976 and was kept under house arrest until the Vietnamese invasion of 1979. Refusing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>'s proposal to co-operate with the Khmer Rouge, as he opposed their genocidal policies, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a> in 1981 and in 1982 headed the Coalition Government of Democratic Kampuchea. He signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a> on 23 October 1991, and a new constitution restored him as king on 24 September 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 6 July 2004 Sihanouk announced in an open letter that he would abdicate again, citing the disregard of his suggestions by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-ranariddh">Norodom Ranariddh</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihamoni">Norodom Sihamoni</a> succeeded him. Sihanouk died of a heart attack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 15 October 2012. He produced about 50 films, beginning with Apsara in 1966, and wrote at least 48 songs. He held more state roles than any other modern royal, and is remembered for leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> to independence, although his reputation was damaged by his association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> in the 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norodom Sihanouk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 9 March 1945 the Japanese overthrew French rule in a coup, interning French troops and officials, and encouraged Bao Dai in Vietnam, <b>Norodom Sihanouk</b> in Cambodia and Sisavang Vong in Laos to declare independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cambodia achieved independence under <b>Norodom Sihanouk</b> on 9 November 1953, though the communist United Issarak Front and the Viet Minh remained active; according to the historian David P. Chandler, leftist Issarak groups aided by the Viet Minh controlled as much as half the country on the eve of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bandung also brought together U Nu, V. K. Krishna Menon, Ho Chi Minh, Zhou Enlai, <b>Norodom Sihanouk</b>, U Thant and a young Indira Gandhi.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>It captured Phnom Penh on 17 April 1975 and proclaimed Democratic Kampuchea, with <b>Sihanouk</b> briefly as nominal head of state until 1976.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-kampuchea">Democratic Kampuchea</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan's coup of March 1945 allowed <b>King Norodom Sihanouk</b> to proclaim the Kingdom of Kampuchea, but France returned that autumn.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-cambodia">French protectorate of Cambodia</a></li><li><blockquote>On 18 March 1970 Cambodia's national assembly, led by Prime Minister Lon Nol and Prince Sisowath Sirik Matak, voted to remove <b>Norodom Sihanouk</b> as head of state while he was abroad.</blockquote><a href="/state/khmer-republic">Khmer Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.57,104.92&amp;year=1980" aria-label="Phnom Penh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7436 1732 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Norodom Sihanouk"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7886 2002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.57,104.92&amp;year=1980">What was here in 1980? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
